From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: a733: let the controller time the R1b busy

`mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` gave this host MMC_CAP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY: a write or an R1b command is
completed only once its bus side is done and SMHC_STATUS CARD_BUSY reads idle.
For a write the wake-up is a hardware interrupt, SMHC_RINTSTS bit 13 armed by
SMHC_THLD BCIG, and that half is hardware-proven. For an R1b command there was
no interrupt at all: the driver queued a delayed work at once and polled DAT0
from it, 32 us doubling to 32768 us, the core's own pacing applied to a
register read.

The A733 has the interrupt for that case too, and `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` said so and deferred it
("DELIBERATELY NOT USED ... one new hardware behaviour per board cycle"). This
is that cycle. Nothing is taken away: the DAT0 poll stays, as the fallback and
as the only thing that enforces cmd->busy_timeout.

WHAT THE UM GIVES (a733-um-v0.92, SMHC0-2 register chapter; SMHC1/SMHC2
registers match those of SMHC0, 11.3.7)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- SMHC_CMD 0x0018, 11.3.8.7, p. 439-440:
  bit 16 R1B_RESP: 1 when the response type of the current command is R1b,
  0 otherwise; with STOP_CMD_FLAG set the controller sets it itself when it
  sends CMD12 and clears it again at the next command.
  bit 17 R1B_BUSY_HW_DETECT: 1 enables hardware detection of the R1b busy,
  0 disables it.
  This file's SMHC_CMD defines jumped from BIT(15) to BIT(21); 16 and 17 did
  not exist in it.
- SMHC_INTMASK 0x0030, 11.3.8.13, p. 443: bit 17 R1B_BUSY_CLR_INT_EN.
- SMHC_RINTSTS 0x0038, 11.3.8.15, p. 446: bit 17 R1B_BUSY_CLR, R/W1C, R1b
  busy clear: set once the R1b busy phase is over, or if there was none.
- SMHC_STATUS 0x003C, 11.3.8.16, p. 448: bit 9 CARD_BUSY, the inverted DATA[0]
  line -- unchanged, and still the thing that decides completion.

What the UM does NOT say, and this patch therefore does not assume:
- whether COMMAND_DONE still arrives at the response when R1B_RESP is set, or
  is held until the busy ends. Both are handled; see the bound below.
- whether bit 17 latches at all with R1B_BUSY_HW_DETECT off. Not relied on.
- whether the controller honours a hardware-detect bit on a data command whose
  automatic CMD12 it gives R1B_RESP itself. Not used; see the scope below.

SCOPE -- WHICH COMMANDS
-----------------------
A command with MMC_RSP_BUSY and no data of its own, on the two A733 cfgs
(can_wait_busy) only: CMD6 SWITCH, incl. the cache flush behind every
REQ_OP_FLUSH; CMD12 when the core sends it as a command; CMD38 ERASE, i.e.
every discard; CMD5 sleep/awake. Deliberately not:

- Writes. Their busy is bit 13, it is hardware-proven on the 8 GiB board (patch `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`),
  and bit 17 is not documented to cover it. Untouched.
- The automatic CMD12 after an open-ended write. The controller sets its
  R1B_RESP itself, our auto-CMD23 path usually removes the stop anyway, and
  `mmc-sunxi-stop-the-card-before-tearing-the-data-engine-down` is live on that code. Untouched.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
- Adds SDXC_R1B_RESP BIT(16), SDXC_R1B_BUSY_HW_DETECT BIT(17) (SMHC_CMD) and
  SDXC_R1B_BUSY_CLEAR BIT(17) (SMHC_RINTSTS), each with its UM line.
- sunxi_mmc_request() sets both command bits and unmasks bit 17 for the
  commands above.
- The IRQ handler treats bit 17 exactly as it treats bit 13: it says "look at
  DAT0 now". The completion condition is unchanged -- bus side done AND DAT0
  idle -- so a bit that fires early, late or never cannot make the result
  wrong. When the look finds DAT0 idle the request is completed in the hard
  IRQ, with no work item and no sleep.
- The stale-bit clear at request start, and the one before the manual stop,
  now cover bit 17 as well. They have to: finalize_request writes 0xffff to
  SMHC_RINTSTS, which does not reach bit 17, and a bit left latched from the
  previous command would read as this one's busy end.
- The bound for the case the UM leaves open: a command sent with hardware
  detect arms done_work at cmd->busy_timeout + 1 s. If COMMAND_DONE never
  arrives, the request fails -ETIMEDOUT with the interrupt state in the log
  instead of hanging. It is needed because the busy wait -- and with it the
  enforcement of cmd->busy_timeout -- is started by the done bit, and because
  nothing behind this host would notice: mmc_wait_for_req() waits without a
  timeout, and mmc_mq_timed_out() returns BLK_EH_RESET_TIMER while hsq is on
  (core/queue.c). done_work already existed for the write case and already
  returns without acting once the request has finished, started its busy wait,
  or collected a done or error bit; only its message and the error bit it sets
  branch on data.

WHAT STAYS IN SOFTWARE, AND FOR WHOM
------------------------------------
Nothing that `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait` measured is removed.

- The DAT0 poll (sunxi_mmc_busy_work) keeps every job it had. It is the
  fallback if bit 17 does not fire; it is the only place cmd->busy_timeout is
  enforced; and it still starts at once for an R1b command -- no grace period
  is added, so a busy shorter than one IRQ latency costs exactly what it costs
  today. The interrupt wins only where the poll is already asleep, which is
  where the overshoot is.
- The 10 ms grace and the bit 13 path for writes: untouched.
- The write busy deadlines (1 s, 10 s with hsq): untouched, and still in
  software. SMHC_WR_BUSY_TMOUT is NOT programmed -- see below.

SMHC_WR_BUSY_TMOUT IS STILL NOT PROGRAMMED, AND WHY
----------------------------------------------------------------
UM 11.3.8.40 puts a write-busy timeout at 0x015C, bits 25:0 WR_BUSY_TO_LMT,
reset 0x00FF_FFFF, the actual limit being WR_BUSY_TO_LMT plus one, with its own
interrupt at SMHC_RINTSTS bit 18. It is true that this leaves the hardware
write timeout at its reset value while the driver rebuilds one in software,
and true that SMHC_TMOUT cannot cover it (11.3.8.3: the value has no effect
while the host writes data). It is still not programmed here:

- the UM gives no unit for WR_BUSY_TO_LMT. A timeout limit without a tick has
  no value that can be derived, only guessed, and a guess that is too short
  ends a legitimate write busy early on a soldered eMMC.
- it is already at reset on silicon (register dump on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19:
  WR_BUSY_TMOUT 0x00FFFFFF), so whatever the tick is, 16777216 of them are
  already the standing hardware bound.
- the unit is measurable before it is programmed, and the instrument is
  already in the tree: sunxi_mmc_end_busy_wait() reads SMHC_RINTSTS bit 18 raw
  -- raw status, so no unmasking needed -- and prints it with the elapsed
  microseconds. One write busy that trips bit 18 gives the tick as
  elapsed_us / 16777216. Until that number exists, programming 0x015C would be
  the guess this tree does not ship.

THE VENDOR, READ AS A HINT (radxa/allwinner-bsp cubie-aiot-v1.4.8)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The register semantics above are the UM's. The vendor confirms the bit pair
exists and is used: sunxi-mmc.h:182-183 defines SDXC_R1B_RESP BIT(16) and
SDXC_R1B_BUSY_HW_DETECT BIT(17), and sunxi-mmc.c:3476 writes both into
SMHC_CMD. Where it differs, and what this patch does instead:

1. It arms them in exactly one place, sunxi_mmc_cqe_enable(), as a standing
   register setting for command-queue mode, and never for an ordinary
   request. Outside CQE its R1b handling is the same DAT0 check plus a
   sleeping thread we are replacing here (sunxi-mmc.c:1129-1135,
   sunxi_check_r1_ready_may_sleep()). So the hardware path the UM documents
   for every R1b command is one the vendor leaves unused on the path our
   kernel actually runs. We arm it per command, where the core already told
   us the response is R1b.
2. It writes the bits without R1B_BUSY_CLR ever being consulted: its CQE
   path leans on cqhci and its non-CQE path polls. We use the interrupt, and
   we say in the log when it does not come.
3. It bounds nothing. A command whose done bit never arrives is caught only
   by its generic 5 s transfer watchdog, whose recovery gates the bus clock
   and resets the host (sunxi-mmc.c:87, 270-345). We bound the request itself
   at its own busy_timeout.
4. It ignores cmd->busy_timeout throughout and says so
   (sunxi-mmc.c:1560-1566). Unchanged from `mmc-sunxi-a733-hardware-busy-wait`: we honour it.
5. It does not program SMHC_WR_BUSY_TMOUT either. Agreed with, for the reason
   above rather than by inheritance.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM
------------------------
Not a throughput patch, and the measurements say so before the board does.
On the 8 GiB board, HS400 200 MHz 8 bit, blkdiscard before each run, performance
governor: sequential write 155-160 MiB/s, read 313-319 MiB/s, ~2050 interrupts
per GiB, and request sizes from 256k to 4M show no trend at all. Per-request
overhead is therefore not what bounds sequential writing today, and an
argument of the form "less overhead per request" is refuted by that flat
curve before it is made. R1b commands are not on that path in any number: a
write stream carries one CMD6 cache flush per REQ_OP_FLUSH, not one per
request.

What it claims is narrower and is about latency and determinism of one class
of command: the end of an R1b busy is taken from the interrupt that reports
it, instead of from a sleep whose granularity grows to 32768 us. The busies
that class contains are the long ones -- an erase, a cache flush -- so the
overshoot removed per command is bounded by the poll's own step, up to tens of
milliseconds, and it stops depending on where in the backoff the busy ended.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
Applies at --fuzz=0 on the full series from a freshly unpacked 6.18.36.
drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.o compiles clean at W=1. NOT hardware-proven:
nothing in this header has been observed on silicon with this patch.

PENDING -- THE OBSERVABLES, AND WHAT REFUTES THIS
-------------------------------------------------
Test kernel with CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG (pinned) and the per-function scope this
tree requires:

  dyndbg="func sunxi_mmc_end_busy_wait +p"

1. Each R1b busy prints how it ended. Expected "cmd 6: busy end by r1b
   busy-clear irq after X us" and "cmd 38: ... r1b busy-clear irq ...".
   FALSIFIER: the same lines keep saying "by DAT0 poll", and/or the
   ratelimited "cmd N: busy ended without the r1b busy-clear interrupt"
   appears in journalctl -k -b. Then bit 17 does not fire on this silicon with
   these bits armed, the patch is refuted and withdrawn -- the software path
   is still carrying the wait, which is why that is safe to find out.
2. Discard latency, the biggest single R1b busy available and already part of
   the bench ritual: time blkdiscard over a fixed raw region, ten runs, before
   and after. Expected: the spread shrinks; the median may barely move.
   FALSIFIER: the spread does not shrink, i.e. the poll was never the thing
   that decided when the discard ended.
3. No regression on the write path, which this patch must not touch: zero
   "write: busy ended without the busy-clear interrupt", zero "card still
   busy", zero "busy clear without DTC/ACD", zero "error -110", and sequential
   write still 155-160 MiB/s under the same conditions.
4. Nothing new in the log from the new bound: zero "cmd N: no command done
   with r1b hardware busy detect armed". One such line means the controller
   holds COMMAND_DONE for the busy, which would be a real finding about the
   silicon and an issue of its own -- and the request still failed cleanly
   instead of hanging, which is the point of the bound.
5. Free, on the same run: any "write busy timeout bit set,
   rint ..." line gives SMHC_WR_BUSY_TMOUT's tick as elapsed_us / 16777216.

Rebased onto `mmc-sunxi-stop-the-card-before-tearing-the-data-engine-down` (2026-09-22). `mmc-sunxi-stop-the-card-before-tearing-the-data-engine-down` made the manual stop clear
SMHC_RINTSTS with 0xffff instead of the write busy clear alone; the R1b
busy clear (bit 17) lies above that half, so the stop now clears
0xffff | SDXC_R1B_BUSY_CLEAR. No other hunk changed.

Assisted-by: Claude
